CAT = "cfpConduct"; SEG = "CON"
# A. Professional Conduct and Regulation (2021 Principal Knowledge Topics A.1-A.6).
# Facts from CFP Board's Code of Ethics and Standards of Conduct and Procedural Rules.
Q = [
("Under CFP Board's Standards, the fiduciary duty applies whenever a CFP professional:",
 "Gives a client financial advice", ["Holds client assets in custody", "Is paid on a fee-only basis", "Signs a written engagement"],
 "The fiduciary duty attaches to every provision of Financial Advice to a client, whatever the compensation method. Custody and a written engagement are not the trigger."),
("Which three duties make up the fiduciary duty in CFP Board's Standards?",
 "Loyalty, care, follow instructions", ["Loyalty, prudence, disclosure", "Care, suitability, diligence", "Honesty, privacy, competence"],
 "The Standards define the fiduciary duty as the Duty of Loyalty, the Duty of Care and the Duty to Follow Client Instructions. Suitability is a lower broker standard, not one of the three."),
("A planner recommends a fund that pays her firm a 12b-1 fee. Before giving the advice she must:",
 "Disclose it and get consent", ["Waive the fee for this client", "Avoid the fund entirely", "Tell the client after the sale"],
 "A material conflict may be managed rather than avoided: the planner must disclose it in enough detail for the client to understand it and obtain informed consent before the advice, then manage it."),
("Under the Standards, when does a CFP professional have to follow the Practice Standards for the planning process?",
 "When financial planning is provided", ["Only for clients over $1 million", "Only when the client pays a fee", "Whenever a product is sold"],
 "The seven-step process applies when the professional provides or agrees to provide Financial Planning, or the client reasonably believes it will be provided. Fees and account size are not the test."),
("What is the first step of CFP Board's seven-step financial planning process?",
 "Understanding the client", ["Identifying and selecting goals", "Analyzing the current course", "Presenting the recommendations"],
 "The steps run: understand circumstances, identify and select goals, analyze the current course and alternatives, develop, present, implement, then monitor and update."),
("A planner's firm earns a commission on one insurance sale a year. May the planner call herself fee-only?",
 "No, any commission rules it out", ["Yes, if commissions are under 10%", "Yes, if the client is told in writing", "Only for her advisory clients"],
 "Fee-only is allowed only when neither the professional nor the firm or related parties receive Sales-Related Compensation. A single commission removes it, whatever is disclosed."),
("Within how many calendar days must a CFP professional notify CFP Board of being charged with a felony?",
 "30", ["10", "60", "90"],
 "The Standards require written notice to CFP Board within 30 calendar days of a reportable event such as a felony charge. The 90-day period is for updating client disclosures."),
("A planner lacks the knowledge to advise on a client's stock options. The Standards let her:",
 "Bring in a competent professional", ["Advise anyway with a disclaimer", "Charge less for that topic", "Skip it without telling the client"],
 "Under the Duty of Care, a professional who is not competent in an area must gain competence, get help from a competent professional, limit or end the engagement, or refer the client."),
("Which is a sanction CFP Board may impose after a disciplinary hearing?",
 "Public censure", ["A civil fine paid to the client", "Loss of a state insurance license", "A FINRA bar from selling"],
 "CFP Board's Procedural Rules list private censure, public censure, suspension, revocation and bars on certification. Fines payable to clients and licensing actions belong to courts and regulators."),
("A client asks for tax return preparation, which the planner will not do. Under the Standards the planner must:",
 "Tell the client it is excluded", ["Prepare it to keep the client", "Say nothing unless asked", "Bill for it as a referral"],
 "A CFP professional must describe to the client any requested services that will not be provided, so the scope of the engagement is clear."),
("A financial advisor who manages clients' money for pay and is not exempt must generally register under the:",
 "Investment Advisers Act of 1940", ["Securities Act of 1933", "Investment Company Act of 1940", "Trust Indenture Act of 1939"],
 "The Investment Advisers Act of 1940 governs people who advise others on securities for compensation. The 1933 Act covers new issues and the Investment Company Act covers funds."),
("Regulation Best Interest, adopted by the SEC, sets the conduct standard for:",
 "Broker-dealers recommending securities", ["Registered investment advisers", "Bank trust departments", "Insurance agents selling term life"],
 "Regulation Best Interest applies to broker-dealers when they recommend securities transactions or strategies to retail customers. Investment advisers already owe a fiduciary duty under the Advisers Act."),
("Which federal law gives consumers the right to dispute errors on their credit reports?",
 "Fair Credit Reporting Act", ["Truth in Lending Act", "Equal Credit Opportunity Act", "Fair Debt Collection Practices Act"],
 "The Fair Credit Reporting Act governs credit bureaus and lets consumers dispute inaccurate information. TILA covers cost-of-credit disclosure and ECOA bans credit discrimination."),
("A planner paid to give investment advice to a company's 401(k) plan is held to the fiduciary rules of:",
 "ERISA", ["Regulation Best Interest", "Uniform Securities Act", "State insurance codes"],
 "ERISA imposes its own fiduciary duties of loyalty and prudence on those who exercise discretion over plan assets or give investment advice to a plan for a fee."),
("Under the Standards, privacy notices to clients about non-public information are generally sent:",
 "At engagement and at least yearly", ["Only when a breach happens", "Every five years", "Only on the client's request"],
 "The Standards require written notice of privacy policies at the start of the engagement and at least once in any 12-month period, unless the policies have not changed and information is not shared."),
("Which statement about a conflict of interest is true under CFP Board's Duty of Loyalty?",
 "The duty outlives disclosure", ["Written consent is always required", "Oral disclosure is never valid", "A disclosed conflict ends the duty"],
 "Disclosure and informed consent let a professional manage a conflict, but the duty to act in the client's best interest continues. Oral disclosure counts and written consent is not required."),
]
